WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.950 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  best-selling author james rebanks has  been called england's most famous farmer  his latest book pastoral song is a love  story dedicated to the land his family  has farmed for generations it's also the  story of how he's discovering a path to  sustain that land and a way of life that  millions of farmers around the world  know and love  we visited him in the lake district of  england and saw this author and shepherd  in action  [Music]  this pocket of countryside in northwest  england just south of the scottish  border is a wonder to behold  dramatic lakes majestic rolling hills  and timeless land and livestock cared  for by shepherds like james rebanks  literally next week when i go to bring  my sheep down from the mountain i'll be  doing the same work with the same  technology as sheepdog as somebody has  done six times a year every year for at  least a thousand years maybe for four  and a half thousand years come on come  on there's one first one i mean this is  mind blowing it's still mind-blowing to  me even though i grew up with it my  sheep are literally the same flock of  sheep that the viking had  a thousand years ago they're the  daughters of the daughters of the  daughters of the daughters right there's  almost nowhere in the world where the  farming is the same thing  despite that remarkable unbroken chain  rebank says what's often missing from  tourist views and classic depictions of  this land  are stories of the people who've  actually worked it  the farmers and laborers  this is an area that's been written  about endlessly but you said you never  saw  your dad and your grandfather in those  books a lot of that stuff was  a sort of upper middle class english  literary culture  and it occasionally mentioned shepherds  occasionally it often speaks about how  beautiful the landscape is  but in terms of the sort of  working-class shepherding culture and  who these people really were and what  they thought their lives were about i  just thought the whole thing like where  is it i thought oh it isn't there is it  someone like me has to write that story  put it into that put it on the shelf for  those other books so that my people are  in this story  like many farm children rebanks dropped  out of school as a teenager to work the  land in his twenties he went back to  school at oxford but after graduation he  returned  six years ago rebanks released the  shepherd's life modern dispatches from  an ancient landscape  now he's followed that up with pastoral  song a farmer's journey which details  the dramatic changes rebanks has made to  his land and way of life  morning ladies  what is farming today  for me farming right now is  it's about can i produce good nutritious  food to contribute to feed all of us so  that you're fed and i'm fed and our  children are  can i make a living those two things are  vitally important of course but they  were the only two things for 50 or 60  years after the second world war and  what we're now realizing is that there's  a bunch of other things that matter  can i look after my soil properly can i  look after the rivers that flow through  my land or off my land properly can i  create the native habitats that we need  around our land you can no longer be the  guy who says no my job's just to produce  cheap food don't talk to me about soil  that doesn't work anymore we're in big  trouble if we don't connect these things  back up  rebanks has reversed many of the  adjustments made on this land over the  past two generations when industrial  farming took over so my grandfather  30 40 years ago thinks  growing thorn on your land is is sheer  insanity like only somebody that went to  college could be that stupid that  includes unstraightening rivers so they  zig and zag more naturally and  rebuilding hedgerows in the process  providing a sanctuary for the birds and  bees that had slowly been disappearing  we have to put back the native habitats  that we need in these landscapes if we  want there to be lots of birds a lot of  insects and lots of other biodiversity  but those insects and those tiny birds  aren't feeding people  no they're not feeding people but we  need them why because we don't have  pollinators for our crops um because we  don't have the biodiversity so you start  getting  where you have monocultures you start to  have really big problems monocultures  only growing one crop at one time in one  field can lead to a boom in production  but the constant use of chemicals to  combat the inevitable bust that follows  is something rebanks believes must end  you're down to almost no fertilizer we  don't use any artificial fertilizer at  all zero and that's changed the farm how  that's changed the farm because i'm not  buying that stuff so agriculture is full  of people pulling up to farm gates and  trying to sell you things that sound  like a great idea but you have to write  them a check and it's required us to  re-learn how to grow grass really well  using the sunshine and the rain rather  than  fossil fuels  rebank's strong opinions have at times  put him in odds with his ancestors who  he writes about lovingly but also  critically  when i read your first book you kept  talking about your grandfather  and i kept wondering this guy doesn't  like his dad  [Laughter]  that's very perceptive so when i was  when i was 20 years old i was gonna my  first ideas about writing a book was i  was going to write an angry young man  book um what actually happened was i was  nearly 40 by the time i wrote the book  and  that anger had gone like me and my dad  had got through whatever problems we had  in my early 20s  when i when i ultimately wrote finished  that book my dad was dying of cancer and  i loved him very much and i respected  him very much and he'd grown up too we  were two different people 20 years later  and then in the second book i mean he  emerges as  more of a hero  yeah i i think so and then also looking  back i realize now  how horrible i probably was as an 18 or  20 year old  no and it must have been  a complete jackass to be the father of  so  so yeah i think this is all part of  growing up as and i now thank my thank  the stars that i didn't write the angry  young man book about my dad because  because that's not what i i want i guess  it's better to hate your father at first  and then learn to love him versus that's  right when my when my father died six  years ago  uh there was a one of the last  conversations i had with him i'd like  looked him in the eye and i said you and  me okay and he said yeah we're  absolutely right  and  there was no unfinished business  [Music]  there may be no unfinished business  between him and his dad  but work is never finished on the farm  as rebanks ponders what this place will  look like once his stewardship is  complete  do you consider yourself now  a writer or a farmer i've always thought  i was a farmer that writes and then  i think it's changing uh  i can see the day where i might not be  the most important person on this farm  one of my daughters or one of my sons  will will take it on and maybe my  responsibility was to carry it that  little way and then hand it on and  that's great that's really important to  me and it's a lot more important to me  that my my kids think i'm a good man or  i looked after our land properly than  than that i'm some sort of hot shot  author or something  to me being quietly decent and working  hard at the things you care about those  are probably the things i admire in  people you like being a hot shot author  though  a little bit  he really does right who wouldn't  it's great to see him so focused on the  future so government subsidies have long  been a big part of farming both in  europe and here he says those need to  continue but may be adjusted so that  it's funds farms that are a little more  sustainable moving forward also the  consumer he says has a responsibility to  be asking more questions about where  their food comes from anonymous meat not  necessarily a good thing more farmers  need to take on that contagion that  enthusiasm about the original land  thanks jeff well

